# TASK B1 – IMPLEMENT AND INSTRUMENT THE RECURSIVE FUNCTIONS

## B1.1 – Validate the Implementation (2 Marks)

The digital root and power functions were implemented using recursion. The functions were tested with ordinary values, zero, one and a larger number.

### Test Table

| Test | Input | Expected result | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| Digital root | 0 | 0 | Checks zero input |
| Digital root | 1 | 1 | Checks single-digit input |
| Digital root | 9875 | 2 | Checks repeated digit addition |
| Power | 2^0 | 1 | Checks base case |
| Power | 2^1 | 2 | Checks exponent one |
| Power | 2^30 | 1073741824 | Checks a larger exponent |

**Explanation:** All these tests verify that the recursive functions handle base cases and ordinary calculations correctly.

## B1.2 – Compare with Your Design (1 Mark)

The C implementation follows the pseudocode designed in Part A.

The digital root function uses a base case when the number is less than 10. Otherwise, it calculates the sum of digits and calls itself again.

The fast power function uses the exponent-halving method. It calculates the half power once and squares the result. If the exponent is odd, it multiplies by the base.

A call counter was added to measure the number of recursive function calls.

## B1.3 – Count the Calls (2 Marks)

The linear recursive power function reduces the exponent by one in each call.

The fast recursive power function reduces the exponent by half in each call.

### Experimental Call Count Table

| Exponent | Linear recursive calls | Fast recursive calls |
|---|---:|---:|
| 10 | 11 | 5 |
| 100 | 101 | 8 |
| 1,000 | 1,001 | 11 |
| 10,000 | 10,001 | 15 |

### Recurrences

**Linear power:**

T(n) = T(n-1) + 1

Number of calls = n + 1.

Time complexity = O(n).

**Fast power:**

T(n) = T(floor(n/2)) + 1

Number of calls = approximately O(log n).

Time complexity = O(log n).

**Observation:** Fast recursive power requires significantly fewer function calls than linear recursive power as the exponent increases.

## B1.4 – Find the Limit (2 Marks)

The linear recursive power function makes one recursive call for every reduction of the exponent.

When the exponent becomes very large, the call stack consumes more memory. If the available stack space is exceeded, the program may terminate due to stack overflow.

The exact failure limit depends on the compiler, operating system, stack configuration and function implementation.

### Iterative Comparison

The iterative power function uses a loop instead of recursive function calls.

It does not create a growing recursive call stack. Its extra space complexity is O(1), while the linear recursive method uses O(n) stack space.

**Experimental record:** Run the program on the target computer and record the largest successful exponent and the first failing exponent, if a failure occurs. Do not assume a universal crash value.

## Complete the Code – TODO 1

The `power_fast()` function contains:

```c
if (exp == 0)
    return 1;

half = power_fast(base, exp / 2);

if (exp % 2 == 0)
    return half * half;

return base * half * half;
```

The half-power is calculated only once and reused. This avoids unnecessary recursive calls.

### Additional Experiment

If the following expression is used:

```c
return power_fast(base, exp / 2) *
       power_fast(base, exp / 2);
```

the same recursive calculation is performed twice at each level.

For exponent 30, the recursive call count becomes 63 instead of 6.

**Observation:** Reusing the result stored in `half` avoids repeated calculations and improves efficiency.

## Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

An iterative square-and-multiply method can calculate power without recursion.

| Method | Time complexity | Stack space |
|---|---|---|
| Linear recursive power | O(n) | O(n) |
| Fast recursive power | O(log n) | O(log n) |
| Iterative square-and-multiply | O(log n) | O(1) |

### Engineering Decision

Fast recursive power is much more efficient than linear recursive power because it reduces the exponent by half.

For applications where stack memory should be minimized, iterative square-and-multiply is preferable because it provides O(log n) time with O(1) extra space.

### Conclusion

